{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-134038",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find out how much juice Mark bought in total, we have to calculate the following sum:\n\n$$\n3+\\frac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nTo add $3$ and $\\frac{7}{4},$ we need to express the whole number $3$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $4.$\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThen we put this fraction over a denominator of $4$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 4}{1\\times 4}=\\frac{12}{4}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}=\\frac{12+7}{4}=\\frac{19}{4}\n$$\n\nExpressing our answer as a mixed number, we have\n\n$$\n19÷4=4\\,\\text{R}\\,3=4\\,\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mark bought $4\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ liters of juice in total.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
